  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1149 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThere is decent agreement in the mid-range models that a trough \ntraversing the NRN plains will suppress the ridge across the Cen MS \nRiver valley and parts of the Mid West while pushing a cold front \nsouth into the Panhandles by Wed. The high center will then reform \nacross the Desert SW leaving NW flow in place across the area by \nThu. With H7 moisture axis building across the area from the west, \nthis provides two potential days for more widespread convection in \nthe area (Wed and Thu). Models seem more focused on Wed for best \nPOPs mainly due to the front and moisture, but the position of the \nH5 ridge and 596DM heights across the area is a bit concerning \ndespite the high moisture. That said, there is potential for a weak \nS/WV in the aftn/eve to work into the area, especially if the more \nagressive ECMWF verifies, and this along with the front and PWs \nexceeding 1.5 inches should induce at least some storms. By \nThursday, the synoptic setup is much better with weaker ridging \nand stronger S/WV within a better established NW flow. That said, \nlocation of the boundary may not be as favorable (we shall see), \nand if we manage to convect more widespread on Wednesday, \natmosphere could be in recovery mode which always increases \nuncertainty. It feels safe to say that one of the two days will \nhave potential for a more organized convective event, but having \nsignificant activity on both days might be a stretch. Which will \nbe the better day is difficult to determine this far out in time.\nMain threats from storms will be locally heavy rainfall and strong\ndownburst winds. The NBM for this 2-day period indicates a 20-30%\nchance for 1/2\" or more total rainfall across the northern \nPanhandles and a 10-20 percent chance across the south. As we saw \nlast Tuesday, this does not guarantee the highest QPF will be in \nthe north, but that is where majority of the models show it to be \ncurrently.\n\nThe mid-week front is not expected to make significant progress \nsouth of the area before retreating and NBM only briefly and \nmodestly decreases highs. In fact the NBM maintains above normal \ntemps the entire extended period. NBM also maintains a low POP over \nthe weekend, but ensemble solutions are all over the map after mid-\nweek and confidence is low. Models continue to hint at the first \nsignificant front of the season the following week, but timing is \nanywhere from late Sunday (Sep 13th) to the middle of the week \ndays later (or not at all). One thing is certain, the first big \nfront of the fall is going to get lots of attention given the long\nand hot summer the region has experienced.",
